IMPORT prelude
IMPORT `qld-provisions`

-- Acts Interpretation Act 1954 (Qld): the cross-references, as data.
-- Every reference in the text from one provision to another provision of the same text is one row.
-- The rows were found by a script that reads the text file: it looks for the words section, subsection, paragraph, subparagraph,
-- part, division and schedule followed by a number or letter, for "this section", "this subsection", "this part" and "this division",
-- and for "definition <term>" after a cited section or schedule.
-- Words inside single quotation marks (‘like this’) are not read as references, and the illustrations in ss 32F, 35B and 35D and the
-- illustrations of a "definition" in Schedule 1 are not read as references, because they cite provisions that are made up.
-- A reference such as "subsection (1)" or "paragraph (a)" is resolved as s 35B(5) says: to the subsection or section where it occurs;
-- "paragraphs (a) to (c)" next to "subsection (2)" in the same sentence is read as paragraphs of that subsection.
-- "heading of the cited provision" is "" where the text prints no heading (a subsection, paragraph or subparagraph).
-- A reference to another Act or instrument is a row of its own kind and is not resolved here.

§ `The cross-references`

DECLARE `Cross-reference` HAS
    `citing provision` IS A STRING
    `words of the citation as printed` IS A STRING
    `cited identifier` IS A STRING
    `heading of the cited provision as printed` IS A STRING
    `line of the text file` IS A NUMBER

-- Cross-reference OF citing provision, words as printed, cited identifier, heading of the cited provision, line of the text file
`the cross-references within the Act` MEANS LIST
    (`Cross-reference` OF "section 7(2)", "subsection (1)", "section 7(1)", "", 33),
    (`Cross-reference` OF "section 9(1A)", "subsection (1)(a)", "section 9(1)(a)", "", 41),
